  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how functional nanomaterials can be designed by combining molecular building blocks, nanofabrication methods, and AI-guided optimization. At the top, small molecular fragments with desired photophysical traits—such as absorption, emission, brightness, reactive oxygen generation, photothermal conversion, and multifunctionality—are fed into an AI-assisted design workflow. That workflow appears to use learning, modeling, and prediction to generate an “AIgen” library of candidate molecules.\n\nThese molecules are then incorporated into many nanoscale platforms, including polymeric, lipid, silica, rare-earth, metal–organic, and carbon-based systems, as well as nanogels, 2D materials, proteins, cell membranes, and exosomes. The figure emphasizes several fabrication routes, especially self-assembly, high-throughput microfluidics, and coextrusion, showing how simple components are turned into more complex nanoparticle-like structures.\n\nThe lower part highlights engineering after assembly. Molecules can be chemically linked to biological ligands such as peptides, antibodies, DNA, or phages through common coupling reactions, and nanoparticle surfaces can be modified with clickable functional groups. It also shows “smart responsiveness,” where stimuli trigger changes in aggregation or assembly state, leading to controlled disassembly or release. Overall, the image conveys an integrated pipeline from molecule design to responsive nanoengineering.",
